Thank you @Jibbarth. The problem is real: `getConfigForPath()` splits on `.`, so any `useAttributeAsKey()` node keyed by something containing a dot has been unreachable.

I reworked the resolution rather than adding syntax, and pushed it rebased on 8.2. `options.option.main` now just works, no brackets:

```php
for ($i = 1; $i <= \count($steps); ++$i) {
    $key = implode('.', \array_slice($steps, 0, $i));

    if (\array_key_exists($key, $config) && null !== $found = self::resolvePath($config[$key], \array_slice($steps, $i))) {
        return $found;
    }
}
```

Two reasons for preferring this over `path.[sub.path].option`:

Discoverability. Nobody types brackets on a hunch, and the help text is the only place the syntax exists.

More concretely, brackets contradict the command's own completion: `buildPathsCompletion()` flattens paths with dots and emits no brackets, so tab-completion already suggests `test.options.option.main`, and with the bracket syntax accepting that suggestion still fails. Resolving the dotted form makes the suggestions the command already produces work.

On the cost, the naive worry is that joining consecutive steps is exponential in the number of segments. It is not, because `array_key_exists()` prunes at every level: only keys that exist are followed. A path with no dotted keys matches on the first iteration at each level, so it behaves exactly like the previous loop.

Ambiguity, if a node had both `a` and `a.b`, is resolved shortest-key-first, which keeps today's behaviour for every existing path.

Your fixture is kept and the tests now assert the unbracketed path resolves, that resolution continues past a dotted key (`options.option.sub.data`), and that an unknown dotted key still reports the original path. I also added the missing FrameworkBundle changelog entry. Reverting to the old loop fails four of them.
